"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"no_diagnostic_value"},{"id":"year_2_2a_life_insurance_future_lifetime_e9dc2dc2_008","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For the future lifetime $T_x$ of a life alive at age $x$, let $S_x(t)=\\mathbb{P}(T_x>t)$. If $S_x(2)=0.9$ and $S_x(5)=0.6$, what is $\\mathbb{P}(2<T_x\\le5)$?","options":["$0.30$.","$0.60$.","$0.40$.","$2/3$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"83822e32e9a19bab6352f234","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_2_2a_life_insurance_future_lifetime_e9dc2dc2_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"A proposed survivor function for the future lifetime $T_x$ of a life alive at age $x$ gives $S_x(1)=0.7$ and $S_x(2)=0.8$, where $S_x(t)=\\mathbb{P}(T_x>t)$. Why is this invalid?","options":["A continuous lifetime requires positive probability at each exact year.","The probabilities must sum to one across the two survival durations.","The second probability must equal the square of the first probability.","Survival beyond year 2 is contained in survival beyond year 1."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ab9fc5781d72b02c4f8a9cf5","time_limit":35,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"no_diagnostic_value"},{"id":"year_2_2a_life_insurance_future_lifetime_e9dc2dc2_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Age at death $T_0$ has survivor function $S_0$. Given $S_0(30)=0.9$ and $\\mathbb{P}(T_{30}>20)=0.8$ for the remaining lifetime of a life alive at 30, what is $S_0(50)$?","options":["$0.80$.","$0.70$.","$0.72$.","$8/9$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3c5c5b14d3370590346cc43d","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"}]}}